\title{Eliminating FloatLib inline bloat and slow kernel WHNF reductions}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{antigravity}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{performance}
\tag{floatlib}
\tag{lean}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: session [ses-BUYJ](ses-BUYJ), commits \code{\verb~|c099d38~} and \code{\verb~|2d6ba57~}. Lean 4.36.0 and FloatLib 4.1.0.}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Mark FloatLib arithmetic wrappers \code{\verb~|@[noinline]~}.} FloatLib tags \code{\verb~|Model.add~}, \code{\verb~|Model.sub~}, \code{\verb~|Model.mul~}, \code{\verb~|Model.div~}, \code{\verb~|Model.fma~}, \code{\verb~|Model.sqrt~}, and \code{\verb~|Model.convert~} as \code{\verb~|@[macro_inline]~}. Every unguarded call expands into the full IEEE rounding pipeline in the LCNF compiler. Large kernels with many operations hit the 200 000-heartbeat limit during C code generation.}
\li{\strong{Measured speedup.} Placing \code{\verb~|@[noinline]~} on the thin wrappers in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Libc/Ops.lean~} reduces generated C code size by 32x. Every extracted \code{\verb~|binary32~} kernel compiles with default heartbeats, including \code{\verb~|powf~}, \code{\verb~|lgammaf~}, and \code{\verb~|erff~}. Proof reduction stays unchanged because the Lean kernel unfolds \code{\verb~|@[noinline]~} definitions normally.}
\li{\strong{Avoid \code{\verb~|Nat.shiftLeft~} and \code{\verb~|List.foldl~} inside large kernel-checked proofs.} In \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Libc/Microarch.lean~}, symbolic bit shifts via \code{\verb~|Nat.shiftLeft~} expand \code{\verb~|Nat.brecOn~} inside \code{\verb~|omega~} and \code{\verb~|decide~}. Use explicit multiplication by \code{\verb~|2 ^ k~} and \code{\verb~|List.foldl_cons~} rewrites instead. That change keeps every microarchitecture theorem fast in Lean's kernel.}
}
